WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.167
-You know, I saw you quickly

00:00:02.167 --> 00:00:04.267
at the Met Gala
that was here in New York.

00:00:04.267 --> 00:00:05.968
It was a beautiful thing
that raised a lot of money.

00:00:05.968 --> 00:00:08.400
-Yeah, yeah.
-And, man, you were a knockout.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.267
You looked gorgeous.
You stuck to the theme --

00:00:10.267 --> 00:00:11.467
Look at this, Gilded Age.
-Thanks.

00:00:11.467 --> 00:00:14.300
-Look at this dress.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:18.334
I mean, what -- is that --
is that all hand -- hand done?

00:00:18.334 --> 00:00:20.634
-Yeah. One of the coolest things
about this dress is,

00:00:20.634 --> 00:00:22.133
it was built here in New York.

00:00:22.133 --> 00:00:24.734
It weighed 70 pounds.

00:00:24.734 --> 00:00:27.367
-7-0, 70 pounds.
-7-0.

00:00:27.367 --> 00:00:31.267
And it had 25,000 gold
paillettes that were hand-sewn.

00:00:31.267 --> 00:00:33.934
So, I think one of the coolest
things about the Met Gala

00:00:33.934 --> 00:00:37.534
is that it's an opportunity for
designers to dream really big.

00:00:37.534 --> 00:00:40.334
And Joseph Altuzarra
created this gorgeous dress.

00:00:40.334 --> 00:00:42.234
It was brought to life
by all these

00:00:42.234 --> 00:00:44.834
New York-based master craftsmen.

00:00:44.834 --> 00:00:47.434
And it was -- I mean, it's an
honor to wear this beautiful,

00:00:47.434 --> 00:00:48.801
beautiful dress.

00:00:48.801 --> 00:00:51.801
-What is it like walking around
with 70 pounds on you?

00:00:51.801 --> 00:00:54.067
-I napped
for three days afterward.

00:00:54.067 --> 00:00:55.434
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:00:55.434 --> 00:00:57.467
-I did some pilates
in preparation.

00:00:57.467 --> 00:00:59.734
I mean, it was heavy.
I feel like --

00:00:59.734 --> 00:01:02.200
It took us about an hour
to get there

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:03.767
in the car because of traffic.

00:01:03.767 --> 00:01:08.434
So, I was standing so I didn't
disrupt the paillettes.

00:01:08.434 --> 00:01:09.701
And by the time I got there --

00:01:09.701 --> 00:01:10.868
-Wait.
So you're standing in a --

00:01:10.868 --> 00:01:13.133
-I was standing, like --
standing, like, you know,

00:01:13.133 --> 00:01:15.434
just trying not to bite it
on the way there.

00:01:15.434 --> 00:01:16.868
-Wow.
-And then, by the time

00:01:16.868 --> 00:01:19.267
I got there, then you wait
in a line before you

00:01:19.267 --> 00:01:21.667
then walk up about 50 stairs.
-Yeah.

00:01:21.667 --> 00:01:23.934
You really have to have stamina.
-Really got a workout.

00:01:23.934 --> 00:01:25.901
-So you threw down a couple --
ate a couple of power bars.

00:01:25.901 --> 00:01:27.000
-Yeah, exactly.
-"All right, I'm almost there.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.100
Hold on."
-Eat some jerky on the --

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.467
you know, in the line.
-Yeah, exactly.

00:01:30.467 --> 00:01:32.267
And did you get to --
when you went, finally,

00:01:32.267 --> 00:01:34.067
to the Gala, did you walk around
and talk to people?

00:01:34.067 --> 00:01:35.767
-Absolutely not. No.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:35.767 --> 00:01:37.467
I got there, and I was like --
-Laid on the ground?

00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:39.267
-"Thank God,"
and just sat down

00:01:39.267 --> 00:01:40.667
and didn't move
the rest of the night.

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:43.234
At one point,
I braved going to the bathroom.

00:01:43.234 --> 00:01:45.467
And everybody just looked at me
like they felt sorry for me.

00:01:45.467 --> 00:01:47.200
You know,
I was walking so slowly,

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:51.300
and my whole dress was going --
kwk! kwk! kwk! kwk! kwk! --

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:52.767
down the hall of the Met.
-Unh!

00:01:52.767 --> 00:01:56.200
-And then, at some point,
I ran into Lizzo,

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:59.000
who looked incredible.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:00.267
-Yeah, she's cool.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:00.267 --> 00:02:03.033
-And I -- oh, my God.
She looked so amazing.

00:02:03.033 --> 00:02:06.601
I'm such a fan.
I am someone who kind of

00:02:06.601 --> 00:02:11.000
short circuits when I meet my
heroes, and I just saw her,

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.000
and she looked at me,
and she went, "Marvelous."

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.534
And I was like, "I -- you --
you -- oh, my -- you --

00:02:18.534 --> 00:02:22.267
I just -- pfft! -- you are --
you are -- you are --"

00:02:22.267 --> 00:02:24.067
and there was nothing
out the other side.

00:02:24.067 --> 00:02:25.534
I just said, "You are..."
for like 5 minutes.

00:02:25.534 --> 00:02:27.601
-You are, yeah.
"You are."

00:02:27.601 --> 00:02:28.567
-You are.
-You are.

00:02:28.567 --> 00:02:29.634
-You are.
-Yeah.

00:02:29.634 --> 00:02:31.300
-You are.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:32.667
-You just couldn't think
of how to finish it?

00:02:32.667 --> 00:02:34.734
-If she was a fan,
she isn't anymore.

00:02:34.734 --> 00:02:36.367
[ Laughter ]

00:02:36.367 --> 00:02:37.901
But she was lovely.
And she --

00:02:37.901 --> 00:02:39.601
-Do you freak out?
Like, do you get --

00:02:39.601 --> 00:02:42.033
do you, like, fangirl out?

00:02:42.033 --> 00:02:43.968
-Y-Y-Yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:43.968 --> 00:02:47.100
I -- I --
The last person that --

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:51.067
I ran into Jon Stewart
at a Knicks game, who was like,

00:02:51.067 --> 00:02:54.234
"I love the show,"
and I was like, "You are --

00:02:54.234 --> 00:02:57.801
You are -- You are Jon Stewart."

00:02:57.801 --> 00:02:59.167
-"You are Jon Stewart.
Absolutely, you are."

00:02:59.167 --> 00:03:01.334
-"You are him."
-All right, well, Lizzo,

00:03:01.334 --> 00:03:03.801
Jon Stewart, if you're watching,
Rachel's a fan.

00:03:03.801 --> 00:03:05.501
-I --
-And you really are.

00:03:05.501 --> 00:03:06.701
You really are.
-You are.

00:03:06.701 --> 00:03:08.200
You really are.
-You really are.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:09.801
-You are.
[ Applause ]

00:03:09.801 --> 00:03:12.133
-That's all they need to know.
That's all they need to know.

00:03:12.133 --> 00:03:14.767
How are the dogs?
Last time, you were nice enough

00:03:14.767 --> 00:03:17.267
to come on our show through Zoom
'cause of COVID and precautions.

00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:19.467
-Yeah.
-And we did yoga with our dogs.

00:03:19.467 --> 00:03:20.868
-We did.
-Yeah.

00:03:20.868 --> 00:03:23.100
-Which, they were -- mine was
better than yours at yoga.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:24.767
-Yeah, my dog, Gary,
was like, "I'm good."

00:03:24.767 --> 00:03:25.834
-Yeah, Gary was like,
"No, thank you."

00:03:25.834 --> 00:03:27.868
-Yeah, no.
-No, the dogs are good.

00:03:27.868 --> 00:03:31.834
I -- I took them to see
a pet medium recently.

00:03:31.834 --> 00:03:33.300
-Wait. A pet medium?
-Mm-hmm.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:35.434
A pet medium.
So, she promises

00:03:35.434 --> 00:03:39.300
to connect deeply
with your dogs, psychically,

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.801
and then tell you about them.

00:03:41.801 --> 00:03:43.267
So, you know,
I feel like I went into --

00:03:43.267 --> 00:03:44.334
-This is fantastic.

00:03:44.334 --> 00:03:45.434
-Yeah, so I'll give you
her number.

00:03:45.434 --> 00:03:46.634
-I have to do this.
-Yeah, absolutely.

00:03:46.634 --> 00:03:48.467
-So, she can tell you
what the dog's thinking.

00:03:48.467 --> 00:03:50.167
-Yeah, what they're thinking,
what they're feeling,

00:03:50.167 --> 00:03:51.200
what their dreams are.
-Walk me through this.

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:52.334
What happens?
Where is she?

00:03:52.334 --> 00:03:54.000
Where are you?
-So a friend of mine swore

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.400
that this pet medium cured
their cat of flying anxiety.

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:00.667
-Okay.
-And so I thought, you know,

00:04:00.667 --> 00:04:02.501
my pets could certainly
be cured of some anxiety,

00:04:02.501 --> 00:04:04.467
so I should give
this pet medium a call.

00:04:04.467 --> 00:04:06.100
And, you know, I thought,
like, I'm a skeptic.

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.901
I'm not someone --
I should clarify that to this --

00:04:08.901 --> 00:04:11.801
I'm not someone who's like,
"Sign me up for a pet medium.

00:04:11.801 --> 00:04:12.968
That's what I need in my life."

00:04:12.968 --> 00:04:14.400
-Yeah, you need a little
convincing.

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:16.000
-Yeah.
And so, I was like, "Okay.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.234
Yeah, I'll give the pet medium
a try."

00:04:17.234 --> 00:04:21.534
You know, best-case scenario,
I form a deep

00:04:21.534 --> 00:04:24.133
and intimate relationship
with my dogs,

00:04:24.133 --> 00:04:28.234
and worst-case scenario, I have
a great story to tell you."

00:04:28.234 --> 00:04:29.601
[ Laughter ]
And --

00:04:29.601 --> 00:04:31.267
-All right, I want to know
where this kind of lands.

00:04:31.267 --> 00:04:32.434
-Neither of those things
happened.

00:04:32.434 --> 00:04:33.767
-Okay. Okay.
Interesting.

00:04:33.767 --> 00:04:36.300
-And she goes, "Do you have
a question for them?"

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:39.100
-Oh.
-And we're like, "Yeah.

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:41.501
You know, I feel like everybody
kind of wants to know, like,

00:04:41.501 --> 00:04:44.767
what do our dogs think of us?
What do they think of us?"

00:04:44.767 --> 00:04:47.234
And she goes, "Okay."
[ Breathes deeply ]

00:04:47.234 --> 00:04:49.300
-Now, is she holding onto
the dog's paw or anything?

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:51.334
-Well, no, she's on the phone.
-She's on the phone.

00:04:51.334 --> 00:04:52.601
-She's on the phone.
So I'm just imagining that --

00:04:52.601 --> 00:04:54.067
-Wow, she's got a really
good connection.

00:04:54.067 --> 00:04:55.467
Yeah.
-She's on the phone.

00:04:55.467 --> 00:04:57.367
She's got a deep connection.
-Deep connection over the phone.

00:04:57.367 --> 00:04:58.667
Okay.
-She goes --

00:04:58.667 --> 00:05:00.501
-"Let me hear the dog breathe."
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:00.501 --> 00:05:05.000
She goes,
"They think...

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:06.601
you're fine.
They think you're fine."

00:05:06.601 --> 00:05:07.734
[ Laughter ]

00:05:07.734 --> 00:05:09.601
And we were like...
-Like, "Where do I Venmo

00:05:09.601 --> 00:05:10.734
the money to?" Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:10.734 --> 00:05:13.767
-Okay, yeah.
And then we go, "Okay, okay.

00:05:13.767 --> 00:05:15.167
All right. You know,
we're gonna go with this.

00:05:15.167 --> 00:05:18.234
"What do they think
of each other?"

00:05:18.234 --> 00:05:20.200
And she goes, "Mm, mm-hmm.

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:23.701
They...think they're fine."

00:05:23.701 --> 00:05:24.701
[ Laughter ]

00:05:24.701 --> 00:05:26.367
And we were like --
-You're amazing.

00:05:26.367 --> 00:05:28.100
You are amazing.
-"Okay. All right."

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:29.567
And it went on like that.
And eventually we're like,

00:05:29.567 --> 00:05:32.701
"Okay, obviously,
this is B.S."

00:05:32.701 --> 00:05:34.167
So we start asking her
all these leading questions.

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:36.300
We're like, "You know,
we've got to get at least

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:37.767
something funny out of this."
-Yeah.

00:05:37.767 --> 00:05:41.767
-So, one of our dogs, he stares
into space very intently.

00:05:41.767 --> 00:05:44.067
And we've always wondered
if he sees dead people.

00:05:44.067 --> 00:05:45.868
[ Laughter ]
So, we asked her --

00:05:45.868 --> 00:05:47.767
We're like, "She's got to have
something good to say."

00:05:47.767 --> 00:05:50.234
-Yeah, she has to have
a few tricks in there.

00:05:50.234 --> 00:05:53.567
-"Madam pet medium,
does Winston see dead people?"

00:05:53.567 --> 00:05:57.734
And she goes, "He...

00:05:57.734 --> 00:05:59.667
thinks there
could be food there."

00:05:59.667 --> 00:06:01.200
[ Laughter ]
We were like --

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:03.334
-Please do not give me
this woman's number at all.

00:06:03.334 --> 00:06:04.601
Yeah.
-No, but here's the thing.

00:06:04.601 --> 00:06:06.767
If I was a pet medium...
-Yes.

00:06:06.767 --> 00:06:09.901
-...it is a service to humanity,
right?

00:06:09.901 --> 00:06:12.534
People need this kind of hope
in their lives.

00:06:12.534 --> 00:06:14.934
If I was a pet medium,
and people were like,

00:06:14.934 --> 00:06:17.901
"What do my dogs think of me?"
I'd be like...

00:06:17.901 --> 00:06:20.834
-"They love you."
-..."They think you are

00:06:20.834 --> 00:06:24.334
maybe actual royalty.
They've never seen it before.

00:06:24.334 --> 00:06:26.567
They think you are a --"
I mean, what would you say?

00:06:26.567 --> 00:06:29.701
-Of course, I would say,
"Oh, my gosh.

00:06:29.701 --> 00:06:32.167
Both of your dogs
are so impressed

00:06:32.167 --> 00:06:34.567
by how you stand up
on two legs."

00:06:34.567 --> 00:06:35.701
[ Laughter ]

00:06:35.701 --> 00:06:37.734
-Thank you, Jimmy.
-Yeah, you're welcome.

00:06:37.734 --> 00:06:40.067
-And now I feel better.
-Yes, and you feel better

00:06:40.067 --> 00:06:41.400
about yourself.
I'd have a whole list

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:42.734
of fun things like that.

00:06:42.734 --> 00:06:44.767
-And if you're like,
"Does Winston see dead people?"

00:06:44.767 --> 00:06:47.667
I'd be like, "Yes.
Yes, he does."

00:06:47.667 --> 00:06:49.667
[ Laughter ]
-Why not? Yeah.

00:06:49.667 --> 00:06:51.400
-It was a royal disappointment.

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:52.767
And I'll give you
her number anyway,

00:06:52.767 --> 00:06:54.167
in case you want
to give it a go.

00:06:54.167 --> 00:06:55.467
-Yeah, and I'll get a story,
and I'll give you my story.

00:06:55.467 --> 00:06:56.801
-Thank you. Please.

00:06:56.801 --> 00:06:58.400
-I want to talk about this,
'cause I love podcasts.

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:00.868
And this is a scripted podcast
on Audible,

00:07:00.868 --> 00:07:03.467
"The Miranda Obsession."

00:07:03.467 --> 00:07:05.501
This is a true story.
This is a real thing.

00:07:05.501 --> 00:07:08.534
-So, it's inspired
by a true story of this woman

00:07:08.534 --> 00:07:12.033
named Miranda Grosvenor,
who was a model,

00:07:12.033 --> 00:07:14.734
kind of socialite, in the 1980s,
predominantly.

00:07:14.734 --> 00:07:16.367
-Yeah.
-And she would call

00:07:16.367 --> 00:07:20.934
some of Hollywood and sports
and music, sometimes politics,

00:07:20.934 --> 00:07:25.667
most famous men and developed
these intimate relationships.

00:07:25.667 --> 00:07:27.200
Not sexual.
Like, intimate friendships

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:28.968
and things with them
on the phone.

00:07:28.968 --> 00:07:33.501
And not all was exactly
as it appeared.

00:07:33.501 --> 00:07:36.200
And you'll have to give it
a listen to find out why.

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:38.467
-But how did the story get out?

00:07:38.467 --> 00:07:42.734
-So, there was an article
written in 2000 in "Vanity Fair"

00:07:42.734 --> 00:07:44.234
that talked about --

00:07:44.234 --> 00:07:45.834
It interviewed
a bunch of the men.

00:07:45.834 --> 00:07:49.300
You know, Billy Joel,
and I think

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:51.133
Paul Schrader was interviewed.
You know, it's a bunch of these

00:07:51.133 --> 00:07:54.934
men who spoke about their
experiences with Miranda.

00:07:54.934 --> 00:07:56.501
But that story was told largely

00:07:56.501 --> 00:07:58.200
through the perspective
of the men.

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:00.334
A male journalist, you know,
did the research on this.

00:08:00.334 --> 00:08:02.968
It's a really, really brilliant,
interesting article.

00:08:02.968 --> 00:08:04.767
But a team of women --
you know, my partners,

00:08:04.767 --> 00:08:06.334
who are a bunch of women,
we wanted to just dig

00:08:06.334 --> 00:08:08.434
a little bit deeper
into this story and look at it

00:08:08.434 --> 00:08:10.300
through a female lens
and see kind of

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:12.200
why she may have
made these calls,

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:13.701
what prompted her
to pick up the phone,

00:08:13.701 --> 00:08:15.100
and what she gained from it.

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:17.300
So, this story is kind of an
exploration of all those things.

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:18.434
-Congrats on that.
-Thank you.

00:08:18.434 --> 00:08:19.701
-I mean,
"The Miranda Obsession,"

00:08:19.701 --> 00:08:22.300
right there.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:24.133
Let's talk about
"Marvelous Mrs. Maisel."

00:08:24.133 --> 00:08:27.367
-Yeah.
-You know, we love you so much.

00:08:27.367 --> 00:08:29.767
And I've had you on the show
for that great show.

00:08:29.767 --> 00:08:32.868
But now, it's just announced you
got picked up for season five.

00:08:32.868 --> 00:08:36.601
And it will --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:36.601 --> 00:08:39.834
But season five
will be the final season.

00:08:39.834 --> 00:08:41.234
-Yeah.
-Of the series.

00:08:41.234 --> 00:08:42.901
-Yeah.
-Oh, my goodness.

00:08:42.901 --> 00:08:45.367
Now, is that bittersweet,
or do you go, like, "Nope.

00:08:45.367 --> 00:08:47.934
I kind of know
Amy will wrap it up""?

00:08:47.934 --> 00:08:49.868
-Well, both.
You know, it is definitely

00:08:49.868 --> 00:08:52.501
bittersweet but also know that
she's gonna wrap it up.

00:08:52.501 --> 00:08:54.067
It's gonna be an epic
last season.

00:08:54.067 --> 00:08:56.868
And it's gonna be full of
twists and turns and surprises.

00:08:56.868 --> 00:08:58.434
But definitely bittersweet,
yeah.

00:08:58.434 --> 00:09:01.734
I mean, Amy --
Amy let all of us know

00:09:01.734 --> 00:09:03.100
that it was gonna be
our last season.

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:04.868
And I actually saw her
later that night.

00:09:04.868 --> 00:09:07.434
About once a year,
I take her out for Irish coffee,

00:09:07.434 --> 00:09:09.501
I get her wasted,
and I make her tell me

00:09:09.501 --> 00:09:11.801
what's gonna happen
in the next season.

00:09:11.801 --> 00:09:13.701
But this time,
it was more bittersweet.

00:09:13.701 --> 00:09:15.334
No, this time,
we just kind of reminisced.

00:09:15.334 --> 00:09:17.033
And so,
heading into this season,

00:09:17.033 --> 00:09:19.434
I know absolutely nothing
about what's gonna happen.

00:09:19.434 --> 00:09:21.367
-Oh, really?
-Yeah, nothing at all.

00:09:21.367 --> 00:09:24.334
I have no idea.
-I know season four was --

00:09:24.334 --> 00:09:26.801
a lot of it was shot
during the pandemic,

00:09:26.801 --> 00:09:29.367
and the shutdown,
and everyone had to be safe.

00:09:29.367 --> 00:09:31.367
-Yes.
-And I think that you came up --

00:09:31.367 --> 00:09:33.400
or someone came up
with some invention for you

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:34.968
that I've never seen before.

00:09:34.968 --> 00:09:38.634
Because you couldn't
really wear a mask,

00:09:38.634 --> 00:09:40.434
because you wear wigs
in the show.

00:09:40.434 --> 00:09:41.801
-I wear a wig.
Yes, yes.

00:09:41.801 --> 00:09:44.601
-Yeah.
And beautiful hats.

00:09:44.601 --> 00:09:46.334
-Yeah.
-So, but you can't --

00:09:46.334 --> 00:09:48.367
-I couldn't put a mask
over my ears.

00:09:48.367 --> 00:09:50.133
-The lace on the wig.
-Mm-hmm, yeah.

00:09:50.133 --> 00:09:52.200
-So you invented something
called the --

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:53.767
This is the co-brella.

00:09:53.767 --> 00:09:55.601
-Well, I wish I could take
credit for this invention.

00:09:55.601 --> 00:09:57.601
This had nothing to do with me.

00:09:57.601 --> 00:10:00.567
Our COVID team
came up with this solution.

00:10:00.567 --> 00:10:02.434
There's a little --
there's a little HEPA filter

00:10:02.434 --> 00:10:06.133
right here that filters the air
inside my literal bubble.

00:10:06.133 --> 00:10:07.968
I was the bubble girl
for six months.

00:10:07.968 --> 00:10:09.400
-Oh, my gosh!
-Yeah.

00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:11.701
So you would go from your
trailer to the set or wherever.

00:10:11.701 --> 00:10:13.834
-Yeah, trailer to the set,
wherever I needed to go.

00:10:13.834 --> 00:10:15.801
Would hold this little umbrella.
-Every day.

00:10:15.801 --> 00:10:18.200
-As adorable as it looks,
by the end of the season,

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:20.100
I was ready to throw it
into the Hudson.

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:21.901
[ Laughter ]
Or, I'm ready to, you know,

00:10:21.901 --> 00:10:24.300
one day, I feel like,
when future humanoids

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:27.667
dig up our piles of trash
out of the depths of the Earth,

00:10:27.667 --> 00:10:29.033
they are gonna find this,

00:10:29.033 --> 00:10:30.367
and they're gonna have
a lot of questions.

00:10:30.367 --> 00:10:32.000
[ Laughter ]

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:34.300
-Yeah, or just donate it to the
Paley Center just to see, like,

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:38.901
what we did for TV.
-Yes, that's right.

00:10:38.901 --> 00:10:41.267
Yeah.
-I think you're phenomenal

00:10:41.267 --> 00:10:42.801
in this, and I want to show
everyone a clip.

00:10:42.801 --> 00:10:45.367
Here's Rachel Brosnahan
in "The Marvelous Mrs. Maisel."

00:10:45.367 --> 00:10:48.267
Take a look at this.
-No more opening-act gigs.

00:10:48.267 --> 00:10:50.000
-What?
-I will only do gigs

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:51.934
where I can say what I want.
-Midge --

00:10:51.934 --> 00:10:53.400
-That's what Lenny would do.

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:55.067
-That's different.
He's Lenny Bruce!

00:10:55.067 --> 00:10:57.834
-Well, then make me Lenny Bruce.
Make me a headliner.

00:10:57.834 --> 00:11:00.234
-How am I supposed to do that
if I can't even book you?!

00:11:00.234 --> 00:11:01.767
-I don't know.
You're my manager.

00:11:01.767 --> 00:11:05.367
Manage me.
-Miriam, I hear you. I do.

00:11:05.367 --> 00:11:07.100
But you know, as well as I do,

00:11:07.100 --> 00:11:11.601
that's not
how the business works.

00:11:11.601 --> 00:11:13.534
-Then let's change the business.

00:11:13.534 --> 00:11:15.834
-Yeah,
"Let's change the business"!

00:11:15.834 --> 00:11:17.567
Rachel Brosnahan, everybody!

00:11:17.567 --> 00:11:19.868
Season four of
"The Marvelous Mrs. Maisel"

00:11:19.868 --> 00:11:22.033
streaming now on Prime Video.

00:11:22.033 --> 00:11:24.567
And all episodes
of "The Miranda Obsession"

00:11:24.567 --> 00:11:25.901
are on Audible right now.

00:11:25.901 --> 00:11:27.868
Jack Harlow joins us
after the break.

00:11:27.868 --> 00:11:29.133
Stick around.
Come on back.

